ter last accuracy-changing step, measured by truncation-and-resume) via regression R^2. 3) Causal validation of commitment: truncate generation at the probed commitment step, force </think>, and measure accuracy retention; compare against truncating at random earlier/later steps. 4) Localize stop-decision circuit: use attention analysis and activation patching to find heads/layers whose activations most influence termination-token probability; ablate them and measure shift in termination timing and the commitment-stop gap. Test whether commitment readout is preserved when stop heads are ablated (evidence of decoupling). 5) Intervention—Commitment Monitor: training-free early-exit that fires when probe-predicted answer is stable and probe confidence exceeds threshold for k consecutive steps. Evaluate token reduction and accuracy on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA against baselines: fixed truncation, verbosity steering (ASC), RCPD reactive exit, and 'budget forcing'. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup. 6) Generalization: transfer probe/monitor across datasets and model scales (1.5B/7B/8B/14B/32B distills) to test whether commitment is a shared, low-cost readable signal.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'The linear probe for latent answer commitment may be unreliable for open-ended or multi-token answers, limiting to tasks with extractable final answers; mitigated by focusing on math/multiple-choice. Commitment and stop-decision may not be cleanly separable circuits—if patching shows they share heads, the decoupling framing weakens (though this is itself an informative negative result). Probe confidence may correlate with problem difficulty, risking premature exit on hard problems (underthinking); the k-step stability and confidence threshold aim to control this. Steering/early-exit gains may not exceed strong existing baselines; the primary contribution remains the mechanistic decomposition even if the intervention only m