del sizes to confirm sparsity of load-bearing steps. 2) Prospective probing: At each step boundary, extract residual-stream activations *before* the step is generated. Train a linear probe (per layer) to predict the causal label of the upcoming step. Metric: AUROC/accuracy for prospective prediction, compared to (a) a bag-of-words baseline on prior text and (b) a retrospective probe that sees the generated step. Sweep layers to localize where the signal is strongest. 3) Circuit localization: Use activation patching / head attribution to identify components whose activations most determine the probe's decorative prediction; ablate them and test whether the model's tendency to emit decorative steps changes. 4) Router intervention (training-free): During generation, at each step boundary, if the probe predicts 'decorative' with confidence > tau, suppress that step (e.g., steer along the TrueThinking direction toward skipping, or force a step-continuation token) and proceed. Evaluate accuracy and mean tokens on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, fixed truncation, length-aware voting (When More is Less), reactive attention pruning (Think Clearly), post-hoc TTS pruning. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup. 5) Safety/failure analysis: Measure router behavior near the reasoning horizon (final 15-30% of chain) and on hard items where underthinking risk is high; report accuracy drop as a function of tau and analyze false-skip cases. 6) Generalization: Transfer probe across datasets and model scales without retraining to test whether prospective decorative-encoding is a shared, low-cost signal.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'Step boundaries may be ambiguous for models without clean delimiters, complicating labeling; mitigated by using consistent newline/step segmentation and sentence splitting. Resample-ablation labels are noisy proxies for causal influence and depend on the neutral replacement; we will