Response Content: {"total": 1, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "eb3e033db1b13fd002449c901264cb2421417942", "title": "Highlights of the Issue - Large Language Models III", "venue": "Robotics", "year": 2025, "citationCount": 0, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://s-rsa.com/index.php/agi/article/download/15969/11425", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBYND", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.70777/si.v2i6.15969?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.70777/
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "recompute_loop_forgetting",
"Title": "Recompute Loops: Overthinking as Retrieval Failure of the Model's Own Scratchpad",
"Short Hypothesis": "A large fraction of wasted reasoning tokens are spent re-deriving intermediate results the model already computed earlier in the same chain-of-thought. This 'recompute loop' arises because the model fails to retrieve its own prior conclusions from context, and it is mechanistically detectable at the point of recomputation via degraded attention to the earlier result. Restoring that retrieval with a lightweight memory-injection intervention eliminates the redundant recomputation and cuts tokens without hurting accuracy.",
"Related Work": "Prior efficiency work treats redundancy as a scattered-attention or verbosity phenomenon: Think Clearly (2025) prunes low-attention tokens reactively; TTS (2025) labels steps decorative vs. load-bearing post-hoc; NLDD (2026) shows a reasoning horizon where late steps stop mattering; When More is Less (2025) targets a global optimal length. Separately, cognitive-science-flavored interpretability shows LLMs lack persistent working memory (Language Models Do Not Have Human-Like Working Memory, 2025; Self-Attention Limits Working Memory Capacity, 2024; Strong Memory Weak Control, 2025), causing self-contradiction and concept drift. No existing efficiency paper connects these two literatures: we are the first to identify a *specific, structured* redundancy pattern\u2014recomputation of previously-derived intermediate quantities\u2014and attribute it to a *retrieval/working-memory failure* (weak attention back to the earlier result) rather than to generic verbosity or decorativeness. Our intervention (memory injection to restore self-retrieval) is causal and targeted, distinct from content-agnostic pruning or length control.",
"Abstract": "Reasoning models often 'overthink,' but the structure of the wasted computation is poorly understood. We identify a concrete and pervasive pattern we call recompute loops: within a single chain of thought, the model re-derives an intermediate quantity (an equation, a partial sum, a constraint, a candidate answer) that it already established earlier in the same trace. We hypothesize that recompute loops are a symptom of the model failing to retrieve its own prior conclusions from context\u2014a working-memory/retrieval failure\u2014rather than generic verbosity. First, we build an automatic detector for recomputed intermediate results by matching semantically-equivalent value-producing statements across a reasoning trace, and quantify what fraction of tokens recompute loops account for across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3). Second, we test the retrieval-failure hypothesis mechanistically: at the token position where recomputation begins, we measure attention back to the original derivation and compare against matched non-recomputing positions, and use activation patching to test whether injecting the earlier result's representation suppresses recomputation. Third, we turn the diagnosis into a training-free intervention\u2014memory injection\u2014that, upon detecting the onset of a recompute loop, appends a terse reminder of the already-derived result (or steers hidden states toward it) so the model can continue instead of re-deriving. We evaluate token savings at equal accuracy against reactive pruning, verbosity steering, and length-aware baselines, and analyze failure cases. Our work reframes a slice of overthinking as self-retrieval failure and offers a targeted, interpretable efficiency mechanism.",
"Experiments": "1) Recompute-loop detection: Segment CoT into steps. Extract value-producing statements (equations, computed numbers, named intermediate results, candidate answers) via lightweight parsing plus an LLM-judge equivalence check. Flag a statement as a recompute if a semantically-equivalent value was already produced earlier in the trace. Metrics: fraction of steps and tokens attributable to recompute loops per dataset/model size; validate detector precision with human spot-checks on a sample. 2) Prevalence and cost: Correlate recompute-token fraction with total tokens and with 'wasted tokens' (tokens after the last accuracy-changing step, measured via truncate-and-resume). Compare recompute-loop share to decorative-step share (reproduce TTS-style labeling) to show recompute loops are a distinct, non-decorative form of waste. 3) Retrieval-failure test (mechanistic): At the onset token of each detected recompute, measure attention mass directed at the tokens of the original derivation, and compare to matched control positions where the same result is instead referenced/reused (no recomputation). Hypothesis: recompute onsets show significantly lower attention to the earlier result. Sweep heads/layers to localize retrieval heads. 4) Causal patching: At a recompute onset, patch in the residual-stream representation of the original result (from its derivation position) and measure whether the model reuses rather than re-derives (drop in recompute continuation probability). 5) Memory-injection intervention (training-free): During generation, run the detector online; when a recompute onset is predicted (via a cheap probe on the residual stream at step boundaries, trained on labels from step 1), inject a short natural-language reminder of the prior result into the context (or steer toward the retrieval direction) and continue. Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, Think Clearly reactive pruning, verbosity/CREST-style steering, length-aware filtering, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup, and recompute-loop count reduction. 6) Failure analysis: Cases where the model legitimately re-derives to correct an earlier error (verification vs. blind recomputation); measure accuracy impact of suppressing these and tune the injection trigger to preserve genuine re-derivations. 7) Generalization: transfer probe/detector across datasets and model scales without retraining.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "Detecting semantically-equivalent recomputations is nontrivial and may be noisy; we mitigate with parsing plus LLM-judge and human validation, but false positives could over-trigger the intervention. Some recomputation is beneficial (self-correction, verification), so blindly suppressing it may hurt accuracy on hard problems; the trigger must distinguish blind recompute from corrective re-derivation, and this may be hard. The retrieval-failure hypothesis may not hold\u2014recomputation might be a deliberate learned strategy rather than an attention failure\u2014which would be an informative negative result but weaken the mechanistic framing. Memory injection alters the context and could introduce distribution shift or new errors. Gains may not exceed strong reactive-pruning baselines, though the structured diagnosis (recompute loops as a named, quantifiable waste category linked to working-memory limits) is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family specific and biphasic across scales."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'recompute_loop_forgetting', 'Title': "Recompute Loops: Overthinking as Retrieval Failure of the Model's Own Scratchpad", 'Short Hypothesis': "A large fraction of wasted reasoning tokens are spent re-deriving intermediate results the model already computed earlier in the same chain-of-thought. This 'recompute loop' arises because the model fails to retrieve its own prior conclusions from context, and it is mechanistically detectable at the point of recomputation via degraded attention to the earlier result. Restoring that retrieval with a lightweight memory-injection intervention eliminates the redundant recomputation and cuts tokens without hurting accuracy.", 'Related Work': 'Prior efficiency work treats redundancy as a scattered-attention or verbosity phenomenon: Think Clearly (2025) prunes low-attention tokens reactively; TTS (2025) labels steps decorative vs. load-bearing post-hoc; NLDD (2026) shows a reasoning horizon where late steps stop mattering; When More is Less (2025) targets a global optimal length. Separately, cognitive-science-flavored interpretability shows LLMs lack persistent working memory (Language Models Do Not Have Human-Like Working Memory, 2025; Self-Attention Limits Working Memory Capacity, 2024; Strong Memory Weak Control, 2025), causing self-contradiction and concept drift. No existing efficiency paper connects these two literatures: we are the first to identify a *specific, structured* redundancy pattern—recomputation of previously-derived intermediate quantities—and attribute it to a *retrieval/working-memory failure* (weak attention back to the earlier result) rather than to generic verbosity or decorativeness. Our intervention (memory injection to restore self-retrieval) is causal and targeted, distinct from content-agnostic pruning or length control.', 'Abstract': "Reasoning models often 'overthink,' but the structure of the wasted computation is poorly understood. We identify a concrete and pervasive pattern we call recompute loops: within a single chain of thought, the model re-derives an intermediate quantity (an equation, a partial sum, a constraint, a candidate answer) that it already established earlier in the same trace. We hypothesize that recompute loops are a symptom of the model failing to retrieve its own prior conclusions from context—a working-memory/retrieval failure—rather than generic verbosity. First, we build an automatic detector for recomputed intermediate results by matching semantically-equivalent value-producing statements across a reasoning trace, and quantify what fraction of tokens recompute loops account for across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3). Second, we test the retrieval-failure hypothesis mechanistically: at the token position where recomputation begins, we measure attention back to the original derivation and compare against matched non-recomputing positions, and use activation patching to test whether injecting the earlier result's representation suppresses recomputation. Third, we turn the diagnosis into a training-free intervention—memory injection—that, upon detecting the onset of a recompute loop, appends a terse reminder of the already-derived result (or steers hidden states toward it) so the model can continue instead of re-deriving. We evaluate token savings at equal accuracy against reactive pruning, verbosity steering, and length-aware baselines, and analyze failure cases. Our work reframes a slice of overthinking as self-retrieval failure and offers a targeted, interpretable efficiency mechanism.", 'Experiments': "1) Recompute-loop detection: Segment CoT into steps. Extract value-producing statements (equations, computed numbers, named intermediate results, candidate answers) via lightweight parsing plus an LLM-judge equivalence check. Flag a statement as a recompute if a semantically-equivalent value was already produced earlier in the trace. Metrics: fraction of steps and tokens attributable to recompute loops per dataset/model size; validate detector precision with human spot-checks on a sample. 2) Prevalence and cost: Correlate recompute-token fraction with total tokens and with 'wasted tokens' (tokens after the last accuracy-changing step, measured via truncate-and-resume). Compare recompute-loop share to decorative-step share (reproduce TTS-style labeling) to show recompute loops are a distinct, non-decorative form of waste. 3) Retrieval-failure test (mechanistic): At the onset token of each detected recompute, measure attention mass directed at the tokens of the original derivation, and compare to matched control positions where the same result is instead referenced/reused (no recomputation). Hypothesis: recompute onsets show significantly lower attention to the earlier result. Sweep heads/layers to localize retrieval heads. 4) Causal patching: At a recompute onset, patch in the residual-stream representation of the original result (from its derivation position) and measure whether the model reuses rather than re-derives (drop in recompute continuation probability). 5) Memory-injection intervention (training-free): During generation, run the detector online; when a recompute onset is predicted (via a cheap probe on the residual stream at step boundaries, trained on labels from step 1), inject a short natural-language reminder of the prior result into the context (or steer toward the retrieval direction) and continue. Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, Think Clearly reactive pruning, verbosity/CREST-style steering, length-aware filtering, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup, and recompute-loop count reduction. 6) Failure analysis: Cases where the model legitimately re-derives to correct an earlier error (verification vs. blind recomputation); measure accuracy impact of suppressing these and tune the injection trigger to preserve genuine re-derivations. 7) Generalization: transfer probe/detector across datasets and model scales without retraining.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'Detecting semantically-equivalent recomputations is nontrivial and may be noisy; we mitigate with parsing plus LLM-judge and human validation, but false positives could over-trigger the intervention. Some recomputation is beneficial (self-correction, verification), so blindly suppressing it may hurt accuracy on hard problems; the trigger must distinguish blind recompute from corrective re-derivation, and this may be hard. The retrieval-failure hypothesis may not hold—recomputation might be a deliberate learned strategy rather than an attention failure—which would be an informative negative result but weaken the mechanistic framing. Memory injection alters the context and could introduce distribution shift or new errors. Gains may not exceed strong reactive-pruning baselines, though the structured diagnosis (recompute loops as a named, quantifiable waste category linked to working-memory limits) is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family specific and biphasic across scales.'}

