Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "stale_uncertainty_gate",
  "Title": "Stale Uncertainty: Overthinking as a Failure to Update the Internal Confidence Signal That Gates Reflection",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Reasoning models keep reflecting not because they are still genuinely uncertain, but because the internal uncertainty signal that gates continued reasoning is stale: it lags the model's actual, freshly-computable confidence. Overthinking is the gap between a lagging gating-uncertainty and an up-to-date evidence-uncertainty, and forcing an uncertainty 'refresh' breaks the reflection loop.",
  "Related Work": "A wave of 2025 work links reflection to uncertainty: ReflCtrl and SEAL steer reflection/transition thoughts with a latent direction; 'Reasoning Models Better Express Their Confidence' and 'Trace Length is a Simple Uncertainty Signal' correlate calibration and length with an internal confidence state and identify high-entropy 'forking' tokens; 'Calibrating Reasoning with Internal Consistency' and the Verbal Uncertainty Feature (VUF) find linear uncertainty directions. Crucially, ALL of these treat the gating uncertainty as an accurate reflection of the model's current knowledge state, and either steer it down bluntly or use it to early-exit. Our proposal advances a fundamentally different mechanistic claim: the uncertainty signal that drives continued reflection is a STALE, lagging estimate that fails to incorporate evidence the model has already produced. We distinguish two internally-decodable quantities \u2014 a 'gating-uncertainty' (the signal upstream of reflection-onset tokens) and an 'evidence-uncertainty' (uncertainty recomputed over the freshest reasoning state) \u2014 test whether they diverge, show the divergence predicts wasted tokens, and design a targeted 'uncertainty refresh' intervention. This is a lag/update-failure account, not another steering vector or exit rule.",
  "Abstract": "Reasoning LLMs often continue to reflect and verify long after they have effectively solved a problem. Prior interpretability work ties this behavior to an internal uncertainty signal, but implicitly assumes that signal accurately tracks the model's current confidence and simply remains high. We propose a different mechanism: the uncertainty signal that gates continued reflection is stale. It lags behind the confidence the model could compute from its most recent reasoning, so the model keeps triggering verification against an out-of-date sense of doubt. We formalize two residual-stream-decodable quantities: gating-uncertainty, read just before reflection-onset tokens, and evidence-uncertainty, read after conditioning on the freshest reasoning state. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we train lightweight probes for both and test whether gating-uncertainty systematically lags evidence-uncertainty during overthinking, using granger-style temporal analysis and the identified 'forking' tokens. We show the lag magnitude predicts wasted tokens better than absolute uncertainty or trace length. We then localize where the gating signal is read via activation patching, and introduce a training-free 'uncertainty refresh': at reflection-onset, we patch the stale gating representation with the fresh evidence-uncertainty representation, testing whether this suppresses the redundant reflection loop. We evaluate token savings at equal accuracy against ReflCtrl/SEAL-style steering, trace-length exit, and internal-consistency exit, and analyze failures on genuinely hard items. Our work reframes a slice of overthinking as an uncertainty-update failure and offers a targeted, interpretable efficiency fix.",
  "Experiments": "1) Probe construction: Segment CoT into steps and mark reflection-onset tokens (via lexical cues like 'wait/let me check/but' plus a reflection-direction classifier reproduced from ReflCtrl). Train two linear probes on the residual stream to predict eventual answer correctness / calibrated confidence: (a) gating-uncertainty probe read at the token position immediately preceding a reflection-onset, (b) evidence-uncertainty probe read at the end of the most recent completed reasoning step (freshest state). Validate both against held-out correctness (AUROC). 2) Lag test: For each reflection episode, compare gating-uncertainty to evidence-uncertainty at the same point. Hypothesis: during overthinking, gating-uncertainty is significantly higher (more doubtful) than evidence-uncertainty. Quantify the lag as the temporal offset (in steps) by which evidence-uncertainty at step t-k best matches gating-uncertainty at step t (cross-correlation / granger-style). Test whether reflection-onset preferentially fires when the lag is large. Connect to high-entropy 'forking' tokens. 3) Prevalence and cost: Correlate per-trace mean lag with (i) total tokens and (ii) 'wasted tokens' (tokens after last accuracy-changing step via truncate-and-resume). Compare predictive power (regression R^2) of lag against absolute gating-uncertainty, trace length, and internal-consistency score. 4) Localization: Use activation patching to find layers/heads where the gating-uncertainty is read into the reflection-onset decision; ablate and measure change in reflection frequency and lag. 5) Uncertainty-refresh intervention (training-free): At reflection-onset, patch the residual-stream components carrying gating-uncertainty with the fresh evidence-uncertainty representation (computed from the latest step), then continue generation. Measure whether redundant reflection is suppressed. Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, ReflCtrl/SEAL reflection steering, trace-length early-exit, internal-consistency early-exit, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, reflection-count reduction, wall-clock speedup. 6) Failure analysis: On hard items where reflection is corrective, verify refresh does not suppress reflection when evidence-uncertainty is genuinely high; report accuracy drop vs. lag threshold, and characterize cases where the fresh estimate is itself overconfident (underthinking risk). 7) Generalization: transfer probes and refresh trigger across datasets and model scales without retraining.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The distinction between 'gating' and 'evidence' uncertainty relies on read-position choices that may be entangled; if the two probes are not separable, the lag framing weakens (an informative negative result). Confidence probes are noisy for open-ended answers, so we focus on math/multiple-choice with extractable answers. The stale-uncertainty hypothesis may be wrong \u2014 reflection could be a deliberate learned exploration strategy rather than an update failure \u2014 which would still yield a publishable mechanistic characterization. The uncertainty-refresh patch alters internal state and may introduce new errors or overconfidence, causing underthinking on hard items; the evidence-uncertainty threshold aims to guard against this. Intervention gains may not exceed strong steering baselines, but the lag diagnosis is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family specific and vary non-monotonically with scale."
}}
