Response Content: {"total": 27, "offset": 0, "next": 5, "data": [{"paperId": "bcc5304b13840cc407025d72d1bd45b0abe99ebc", "title": "CODA: Difficulty-Aware Compute Allocation for Adaptive Reasoning", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "citationCount": 2, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08659, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the sour
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "difficulty_contraction_failure",
"Title": "Difficulty That Won't Shrink: Overthinking as a Failure of the Internal Difficulty Signal to Contract as Reasoning Progresses",
"Short Hypothesis": "LLMs carry a linearly-decodable, attention-head-mediated 'difficulty' representation. Prior work reads it only once, before generation. We hypothesize this difficulty signal should contract during a chain of thought as sub-goals are resolved and the problem becomes effectively easy, and that overthinking occurs precisely when this contraction fails\u2014the internal difficulty stays high even after the problem is essentially solved. Forcing the difficulty signal to contract (via targeted head steering) at the point where residual work is minimal should curtail redundant reasoning without hurting accuracy.",
"Related Work": "A recent cluster of interpretability work establishes that LLMs linearly encode problem difficulty in pre-generation activations, localize it to specific final-layer attention heads (Probing the Difficulty Perception Mechanism, 2025; A Shared Geometry of Difficulty, 2026; LLMs Encode Their Failures, 2026), and use it to allocate compute at the input level (ACTSC 2026, FR-Ponder 2025, AdaCtrl 2025, CODA 2026, UAB 2026). Critically, ALL of these treat difficulty as a static, pre-generation property of the input and use it to set a budget or sample count before or independent of the actual reasoning trace. None examine how the difficulty representation evolves *during* generation, nor connect overthinking to a failure of that representation to update. Our contribution is orthogonal: (1) we show the same difficulty direction/heads can be read at intermediate points in the CoT and that it normally contracts as work is completed; (2) we identify overthinking with a specific dynamical failure\u2014non-contracting difficulty despite resolved residual work; and (3) we intervene on the difficulty heads mid-generation to induce contraction. This is a temporal-dynamics + causal-intervention account of an established static representation, not another budget-allocation method or steering-vector-for-length.",
"Abstract": "Recent interpretability work shows that large language models linearly encode a notion of problem difficulty in their activations, mediated by specific attention heads, and that this signal can be read before generation to allocate compute. But difficulty is not static: as a reasoning model works through a problem and resolves sub-goals, the *residual* problem should become easier, and its internal difficulty should shrink. We hypothesize that overthinking is a failure of this contraction\u2014the internal difficulty signal remains elevated even after the problem is effectively solved, driving the model to keep reasoning. We first replicate a linear difficulty probe and localize difficulty-sensitive heads on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), then read the difficulty signal at each step of a chain of thought on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA. We test whether difficulty normally contracts as residual work (measured by truncate-and-resume accuracy) decreases, and whether overthought traces exhibit stalled, non-contracting difficulty. We show the contraction-failure metric predicts wasted tokens better than absolute difficulty or trace length. We then causally validate the difficulty heads by patching/steering them mid-generation toward the 'easy' pole once residual work is low, and measure whether this induces early, correct termination. We compare token savings at equal accuracy against input-level difficulty budgeting (CODA/AdaCtrl-style), length steering, and confidence early-exit, and analyze failure cases where genuine difficulty persists. Our work reframes a static difficulty representation as a dynamical control signal and locates overthinking in its update failure.",
"Experiments": "1) Difficulty probe & head localization (replication): Train a linear probe on residual-stream / final-layer activations to predict human/model difficulty (using Easy2Hard-AMC-style labels and empirical pass-rates) and identify difficulty-sensitive attention heads via ablation, reproducing prior findings on DeepSeek-R1 distills and Qwen3. Validate probe AUROC/R^2. 2) Intra-trace difficulty dynamics: Segment CoT into steps. At each step boundary, read the probe-decoded difficulty from the current residual state. Define per-trace difficulty trajectory. Metric: does difficulty monotonically contract, and by how much, from start to termination? 3) Residual-work measurement: At each step, truncate the trace, force termination, resume/answer, and record accuracy \u2014 giving 'residual work' (steps still needed to reach the correct answer). Correlate the difficulty trajectory with residual work: hypothesis is difficulty should track residual work and drop toward zero once residual work is exhausted. 4) Contraction-failure metric: Define contraction failure = area between the difficulty trajectory and the residual-work trajectory after residual work hits zero (i.e., difficulty stays high after the problem is effectively solved). Correlate this metric with (i) total tokens and (ii) wasted tokens (tokens after last accuracy-changing step). Compare regression R^2 against baselines: absolute pre-generation difficulty, trace length, and a confidence probe. 5) Causal head validation: At step boundaries where residual work is low but difficulty remains high, patch/steer the localized difficulty heads toward their 'easy' activation pattern (or add the negative difficulty direction) and measure the effect on termination-token probability and continuation length. Confirm difficulty heads causally gate continued reasoning (vs. control heads / random directions). 6) Intervention (training-free): Online, read step-wise difficulty; when difficulty fails to contract but a lightweight residual-work probe (trained from step 3 labels) predicts low residual work for k consecutive steps, steer difficulty heads toward 'easy' to induce termination. Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, input-level difficulty budgeting (CODA/AdaCtrl-style), length steering, confidence-based early-exit, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup. 7) Failure analysis: On hard items where difficulty legitimately stays high (residual work truly large), verify the trigger does not fire; report underthinking rate as a function of the residual-work threshold. 8) Generalization: transfer probe, head set, and trigger across datasets and model scales without retraining.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "The difficulty direction may entangle with confidence/uncertainty, blurring the distinction from prior 'confidence early-exit' work; we control by comparing difficulty-head steering against confidence-probe steering and require the difficulty heads to be causally distinct. Difficulty may not be cleanly readable at intermediate positions (the probe was trained pre-generation); we mitigate by fine-tuning the probe on intermediate states, but if difficulty is only encoded pre-generation, the dynamical framing weakens (an informative negative result). The truncate-and-resume residual-work measure is noisy and compute-heavy; we subsample steps. Steering difficulty heads could disrupt genuine hard-problem reasoning (underthinking); the residual-work-gated trigger and threshold analysis address this. Intervention gains may not exceed strong difficulty-budgeting baselines, but the dynamical, causal characterization of difficulty contraction is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family and scale specific."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'difficulty_contraction_failure', 'Title': "Difficulty That Won't Shrink: Overthinking as a Failure of the Internal Difficulty Signal to Contract as Reasoning Progresses", 'Short Hypothesis': "LLMs carry a linearly-decodable, attention-head-mediated 'difficulty' representation. Prior work reads it only once, before generation. We hypothesize this difficulty signal should contract during a chain of thought as sub-goals are resolved and the problem becomes effectively easy, and that overthinking occurs precisely when this contraction fails—the internal difficulty stays high even after the problem is essentially solved. Forcing the difficulty signal to contract (via targeted head steering) at the point where residual work is minimal should curtail redundant reasoning without hurting accuracy.", 'Related Work': 'A recent cluster of interpretability work establishes that LLMs linearly encode problem difficulty in pre-generation activations, localize it to specific final-layer attention heads (Probing the Difficulty Perception Mechanism, 2025; A Shared Geometry of Difficulty, 2026; LLMs Encode Their Failures, 2026), and use it to allocate compute at the input level (ACTSC 2026, FR-Ponder 2025, AdaCtrl 2025, CODA 2026, UAB 2026). Critically, ALL of these treat difficulty as a static, pre-generation property of the input and use it to set a budget or sample count before or independent of the actual reasoning trace. None examine how the difficulty representation evolves *during* generation, nor connect overthinking to a failure of that representation to update. Our contribution is orthogonal: (1) we show the same difficulty direction/heads can be read at intermediate points in the CoT and that it normally contracts as work is completed; (2) we identify overthinking with a specific dynamical failure—non-contracting difficulty despite resolved residual work; and (3) we intervene on the difficulty heads mid-generation to induce contraction. This is a temporal-dynamics + causal-intervention account of an established static representation, not another budget-allocation method or steering-vector-for-length.', 'Abstract': "Recent interpretability work shows that large language models linearly encode a notion of problem difficulty in their activations, mediated by specific attention heads, and that this signal can be read before generation to allocate compute. But difficulty is not static: as a reasoning model works through a problem and resolves sub-goals, the *residual* problem should become easier, and its internal difficulty should shrink. We hypothesize that overthinking is a failure of this contraction—the internal difficulty signal remains elevated even after the problem is effectively solved, driving the model to keep reasoning. We first replicate a linear difficulty probe and localize difficulty-sensitive heads on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), then read the difficulty signal at each step of a chain of thought on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA. We test whether difficulty normally contracts as residual work (measured by truncate-and-resume accuracy) decreases, and whether overthought traces exhibit stalled, non-contracting difficulty. We show the contraction-failure metric predicts wasted tokens better than absolute difficulty or trace length. We then causally validate the difficulty heads by patching/steering them mid-generation toward the 'easy' pole once residual work is low, and measure whether this induces early, correct termination. We compare token savings at equal accuracy against input-level difficulty budgeting (CODA/AdaCtrl-style), length steering, and confidence early-exit, and analyze failure cases where genuine difficulty persists. Our work reframes a static difficulty representation as a dynamical control signal and locates overthinking in its update failure.", 'Experiments': "1) Difficulty probe & head localization (replication): Train a linear probe on residual-stream / final-layer activations to predict human/model difficulty (using Easy2Hard-AMC-style labels and empirical pass-rates) and identify difficulty-sensitive attention heads via ablation, reproducing prior findings on DeepSeek-R1 distills and Qwen3. Validate probe AUROC/R^2. 2) Intra-trace difficulty dynamics: Segment CoT into steps. At each step boundary, read the probe-decoded difficulty from the current residual state. Define per-trace difficulty trajectory. Metric: does difficulty monotonically contract, and by how much, from start to termination? 3) Residual-work measurement: At each step, truncate the trace, force termination, resume/answer, and record accuracy — giving 'residual work' (steps still needed to reach the correct answer). Correlate the difficulty trajectory with residual work: hypothesis is difficulty should track residual work and drop toward zero once residual work is exhausted. 4) Contraction-failure metric: Define contraction failure = area between the difficulty trajectory and the residual-work trajectory after residual work hits zero (i.e., difficulty stays high after the problem is effectively solved). Correlate this metric with (i) total tokens and (ii) wasted tokens (tokens after last accuracy-changing step). Compare regression R^2 against baselines: absolute pre-generation difficulty, trace length, and a confidence probe. 5) Causal head validation: At step boundaries where residual work is low but difficulty remains high, patch/steer the localized difficulty heads toward their 'easy' activation pattern (or add the negative difficulty direction) and measure the effect on termination-token probability and continuation length. Confirm difficulty heads causally gate continued reasoning (vs. control heads / random directions). 6) Intervention (training-free): Online, read step-wise difficulty; when difficulty fails to contract but a lightweight residual-work probe (trained from step 3 labels) predicts low residual work for k consecutive steps, steer difficulty heads toward 'easy' to induce termination. Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, input-level difficulty budgeting (CODA/AdaCtrl-style), length steering, confidence-based early-exit, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup. 7) Failure analysis: On hard items where difficulty legitimately stays high (residual work truly large), verify the trigger does not fire; report underthinking rate as a function of the residual-work threshold. 8) Generalization: transfer probe, head set, and trigger across datasets and model scales without retraining.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The difficulty direction may entangle with confidence/uncertainty, blurring the distinction from prior 'confidence early-exit' work; we control by comparing difficulty-head steering against confidence-probe steering and require the difficulty heads to be causally distinct. Difficulty may not be cleanly readable at intermediate positions (the probe was trained pre-generation); we mitigate by fine-tuning the probe on intermediate states, but if difficulty is only encoded pre-generation, the dynamical framing weakens (an informative negative result). The truncate-and-resume residual-work measure is noisy and compute-heavy; we subsample steps. Steering difficulty heads could disrupt genuine hard-problem reasoning (underthinking); the residual-work-gated trigger and threshold analysis address this. Intervention gains may not exceed strong difficulty-budgeting baselines, but the dynamical, causal characterization of difficulty contraction is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family and scale specific."}

