s current implicit final answer via (a) logit-lens at the last position and (b) a trained linear probe on the residual stream predicting the eventual answer token(s). Validate probe against final answer (accuracy/AUROC). Output: a discrete answer trajectory per trace. 2) Oscillation characterization: For each trace compute number of distinct candidate answers, total switch count, dominant switch-pair identity (are switches between just 2-3 candidates?), and fit a damping coefficient to the inter-switch spacing/amplitude over the trace. Report distributions across datasets and model sizes; test whether oscillation is concentrated in a few competing candidates (as in the latent backtracking finding) vs. diffuse. 3) Settling time vs. waste: Define settling time = tokens from first commitment to last switch (the final flip). Measure wasted tokens via truncate-and-resume (tokens after the last accuracy-changing step). Regress wasted tokens on settling time vs. baselines: one-shot stability point, absolute confidence probe, trace length; compare R^2. 4) Danger of one-shot exit: Quantify how often the answer is stable for k steps and then flips (to correct or incorrect), showing one-shot stability exit would have committed to a wrong answer; report expected accuracy loss of naive stability exit. 5) Circuit localization: Use activation patching / head attribution at switch onsets to find heads whose activations drive candidate switching; ablate and measure change in switch count and settling time (test whether switching is head-mediated, connecting to non-retrieval uncertainty heads and cognitive/backtracking heads). 6) Settling detector (training-free): Online, decode step-wise answer; a lightweight probe on the residual stream at step boundaries predicts P(future flip). Early-exit only when answer is stable for k steps AND predicted flip probability < tau (converged/damped regime). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, one-shot k-step stability exit, confidence early-exit, verbosity/manifold steering, fixed truncation. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, missed-flip rate (exits before a beneficial correction), wall-clock speedup. 7) Generalization: transfer probes and settling detector across datasets and model scales without retraining.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'Step-wise latent-answer decoding is only reliable for extractable answers, so we restrict to math/multiple-choice. Oscillation may be rare in some models or datasets, making settling-time a weak predictor (an informative negative result). The future-flip probe may be hard to calibrate and could exit prematurely on hard problems where a late flip is corrective (underthinking); the missed-flip metric and tau tuning address this trade-off explicitly. Switching may not be cleanly head-mediated, weakening the circuit-localization claim though the dynamical characterization stands independently. Damping-coefficient fitting on discrete, short trajectories is noisy; we mitigate by subsampling and reporting robust summary statistics. Detector gains may not exceed strong steering baselines, but the flip-aware settling criterion targets a documented failure of one-shot exit and is a standalone contribution. Results may be model-family and scale specific.'}

