Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "branch_worthiness_signal",
  "Title": "Does a Model Know When to Fork? An Internal Branch-Worthiness Signal for Per-Problem Parallel Compute Allocation",
  "Short Hypothesis": "A reasoning model's early hidden states, from a single greedy prefix, linearly encode a 'branch-worthiness' signal: whether the problem has multiple plausible competing solution paths that make parallel sampling (best-of-N / self-consistency) worthwhile, versus a single-basin problem where extra samples are wasted. This signal derives from the entropy of the model's internal candidate-answer distribution and can be read cheaply to allocate parallel budget per-problem, saving compute at fixed accuracy.",
  "Related Work": "Test-time scaling work allocates parallel budget per-problem using black-box predictors: T2 (2026) estimates optimal parallelism from latent representations; OptScale/OSCA/SCALE/DORA (2024-2025) derive resource-allocation rules; First Finish Search (2025) returns the first-completing trace; Latent-Trajectory signals (Vilas et al. 2025) predict per-trace success from representation dynamics. All of these either predict per-trace quality (post-hoc, needs the trace) or fit an opaque predictor for how many samples to draw. Separately, interpretability work (Distributional Reasoning in LLMs, 2024; How to Think Step-by-Step, 2024) shows middle layers hold a distribution over candidate intermediate/final answers via parallel pathways. No prior work connects these: we hypothesize that the *internal candidate-answer distribution entropy*, readable from a single short prefix before any full trace or sampling, is the mechanistic origin of branch-worthiness, and that it predicts the marginal value of parallel sampling per-problem. This is a mechanistic account plus a causal test (steering the entropy signal changes sampling utility), not another opaque allocation heuristic or per-trace success predictor.",
  "Abstract": "Parallel test-time scaling (best-of-N, self-consistency) improves reasoning accuracy but wastes enormous compute: many problems are 'single-basin' where all samples converge to the same answer, so extra samples add nothing, while a minority are genuinely multi-path where parallel exploration pays off. Existing methods choose the sample count per problem with opaque predictors and offer little mechanistic insight into what determines the value of forking. We hypothesize that a model's early hidden states already encode a branch-worthiness signal, rooted in the entropy of the internal candidate-answer distribution known to live in middle layers, and that this signal predicts the marginal accuracy gain of parallel sampling before any sampling is done. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we (1) measure per-problem parallel-sampling utility (accuracy gain from N=1 to N=k) as ground truth; (2) read the internal candidate-answer distribution from a short greedy prefix via logit-lens and trained linear probes and test whether its entropy predicts branch-worthiness better than prompt-length, difficulty probes, or output-confidence baselines; (3) localize the heads/layers carrying the distribution via activation patching and causally test whether steering the entropy up/down changes the actual utility of sampling; and (4) build a training-free per-problem allocator that reads the signal from a single prefix and sets N adaptively. We compare compute-at-fixed-accuracy against uniform-N, T2-style latent predictors, and confidence-based allocation. Our work reframes 'how many samples to draw' as reading a mechanistically-grounded internal signal.",
  "Experiments": "1) Ground-truth branch-worthiness: For each problem, draw N=k independent samples (temperature-sampled full traces, k up to 16) and record answer distribution. Define parallel-utility = accuracy(best-of/self-consistency at N=k) minus accuracy(greedy N=1), and answer-diversity = entropy of the final-answer distribution over samples. Label problems single-basin (low diversity, no utility) vs multi-path (high diversity, positive utility). Report the distribution of parallel-utility across datasets/model sizes to quantify wasted budget under uniform-N. 2) Internal-distribution readout from a short prefix: Generate only a short greedy prefix (e.g., first 32-128 tokens). At middle layers, decode the internal candidate-answer distribution via (a) logit-lens projection at the last prefix position and (b) a trained linear probe predicting the *set/entropy* of eventual sampled answers. Compute internal-entropy. Metric: correlation/AUROC of internal-entropy vs. ground-truth answer-diversity and parallel-utility. 3) Baseline comparison: Predict parallel-utility from internal-entropy vs. (i) prompt length, (ii) a pre-generation difficulty probe, (iii) output/verbal-confidence after greedy trace, (iv) T2-style black-box latent predictor. Compare regression R^2 / ranking AUROC; key test is whether the cheap short-prefix internal-entropy matches or beats predictors that need full traces. 4) Mechanistic localization: Use activation patching / head attribution to find layers/heads carrying the candidate-answer distribution (connecting to distributional-reasoning findings); ablate them and measure degradation of the entropy readout. 5) Causal test: Steer the identified components to raise or lower internal-entropy on the prefix, then actually run sampling; test whether increasing entropy raises realized answer-diversity and whether decreasing it collapses samples toward one answer (confirming the signal causally reflects branch structure, not a spurious correlate). 6) Adaptive allocator (training-free): For each problem, generate one short prefix, read internal-entropy, and set N via a monotone schedule (single-basin -> N=1; high entropy -> N=k). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: uniform-N self-consistency, First Finish Search, confidence-based allocation, T2-style predictor. Metrics: accuracy, mean samples/tokens, compute-at-fixed-accuracy, and Pareto accuracy-vs-compute curves. 7) Generalization: transfer probe and allocator across datasets and model scales without retraining.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "The internal candidate-answer distribution is cleanest for extractable-answer tasks, so we restrict to math/multiple-choice. Short-prefix entropy may not capture branch structure that only emerges late in reasoning, weakening prediction (an informative negative result, and mitigated by testing several prefix lengths). Internal-entropy may correlate with plain difficulty, making it hard to distinguish from difficulty-probe baselines; the causal steering experiment and the direct comparison to difficulty probes address this. Steering entropy could produce degenerate or off-distribution samples, complicating the causal claim; we report sample validity. The allocator's gains may not exceed strong latent-predictor baselines like T2, but the mechanistic grounding (branch-worthiness as internal candidate-distribution entropy, causally validated) is a standalone contribution. Ground-truth utility estimation with k up to 16 is compute-intensive; we subsample problems and use smaller k for larger models. Results may be model-family and scale specific."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'branch_worthiness_signal', 'Title': 'Does a Model Know When to Fork? An Internal Branch-Worthiness Signal for Per-Problem Parallel Compute Allocation', 'Short Hypothesis': "A reasoning model's early hidden states, from a single greedy prefix, linearly encode a 'branch-worthiness' signal: whether the problem has multiple plausible competing solution paths that make parallel sampling (best-of-N / self-consistency) worthwhile, versus a single-basin problem where extra samples are wasted. This signal derives from the entropy of the model's internal candidate-answer distribution and can be read cheaply to allocate parallel budget per-problem, saving compute at fixed accuracy.", 'Related Work': 'Test-time scaling work allocates parallel budget per-problem using black-box predictors: T2 (2026) estimates optimal parallelism from latent representations; OptScale/OSCA/SCALE/DORA (2024-2025) derive resource-allocation rules; First Finish Search (2025) returns the first-completing trace; Latent-Trajectory signals (Vilas et al. 2025) predict per-trace success from representation dynamics. All of these either predict per-trace quality (post-hoc, needs the trace) or fit an opaque predictor for how many samples to draw. Separately, interpretability work (Distributional Reasoning in LLMs, 2024; How to Think Step-by-Step, 2024) shows middle layers hold a distribution over candidate intermediate/final answers via parallel pathways. No prior work connects these: we hypothesize that the *internal candidate-answer distribution entropy*, readable from a single short prefix before any full trace or sampling, is the mechanistic origin of branch-worthiness, and that it predicts the marginal value of parallel sampling per-problem. This is a mechanistic account plus a causal test (steering the entropy signal changes sampling utility), not another opaque allocation heuristic or per-trace success predictor.', 'Abstract': "Parallel test-time scaling (best-of-N, self-consistency) improves reasoning accuracy but wastes enormous compute: many problems are 'single-basin' where all samples converge to the same answer, so extra samples add nothing, while a minority are genuinely multi-path where parallel exploration pays off. Existing methods choose the sample count per problem with opaque predictors and offer little mechanistic insight into what determines the value of forking. We hypothesize that a model's early hidden states already encode a branch-worthiness signal, rooted in the entropy of the internal candidate-answer distribution known to live in middle layers, and that this signal predicts the marginal accuracy gain of parallel sampling before any sampling is done. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we (1) measure per-problem parallel-sampling utility (accuracy gain from N=1 to N=k) as ground truth; (2) read the internal candidate-answer distribution from a short greedy prefix via logit-lens and trained linear probes and test whether its entropy predicts branch-worthiness better than prompt-length, difficulty probes, or output-confidence baselines; (3) localize the heads/layers carrying the distribution via activation patching and causally test whether steering the entropy up/down changes the actual utility of sampling; and (4) build a training-free per-problem allocator that reads the signal from a single prefix and sets N adaptively. We compare compute-at-fixed-accuracy against uniform-N, T2-style latent predictors, and confidence-based allocation. Our work reframes 'how many samples to draw' as reading a mechanistically-grounded internal signal.", 'Experiments': '1) Ground-truth branch-worthiness: For each problem, draw N=k independent samples (temperature-sampled full traces, k up to 16) and record answer distribution. Define parallel-utility = accuracy(best-of/self-consistency at N=k) minus accuracy(greedy N=1), and answer-diversity = entropy of the final-answer distribution over samples. Label problems single-basin (low diversity, no utility) vs multi-path (high diversity, positive utility). Report the distribution of parallel-utility across datasets/model sizes to quantify wasted budget under uniform-N. 2) Internal-distribution readout from a short prefix: Generate only a short greedy prefix (e.g., first 32-128 tokens). At middle layers, decode the internal candidate-answer distribution via (a) logit-lens projection at the last prefix position and (b) a trained linear probe predicting the *set/entropy* of eventual sampled answers. Compute internal-entropy. Metric: correlation/AUROC of internal-entropy vs. ground-truth answer-diversity and parallel-utility. 3) Baseline comparison: Predict parallel-utility from internal-entropy vs. (i) prompt length, (ii) a pre-generation difficulty probe, (iii) output/verbal-confidence after greedy trace, (iv) T2-style black-box latent predictor. Compare regression R^2 / ranking AUROC; key test is whether the cheap short-prefix internal-entropy matches or beats predictors that need full traces. 4) Mechanistic localization: Use activation patching / head attribution to find layers/heads carrying the candidate-answer distribution (connecting to distributional-reasoning findings); ablate them and measure degradation of the entropy readout. 5) Causal test: Steer the identified components to raise or lower internal-entropy on the prefix, then actually run sampling; test whether increasing entropy raises realized answer-diversity and whether decreasing it collapses samples toward one answer (confirming the signal causally reflects branch structure, not a spurious correlate). 6) Adaptive allocator (training-free): For each problem, generate one short prefix, read internal-entropy, and set N via a monotone schedule (single-basin -> N=1; high entropy -> N=k). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: uniform-N self-consistency, First Finish Search, confidence-based allocation, T2-style predictor. Metrics: accuracy, mean samples/tokens, compute-at-fixed-accuracy, and Pareto accuracy-vs-compute curves. 7) Generalization: transfer probe and allocator across datasets and model scales without retraining.', 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "The internal candidate-answer distribution is cleanest for extractable-answer tasks, so we restrict to math/multiple-choice. Short-prefix entropy may not capture branch structure that only emerges late in reasoning, weakening prediction (an informative negative result, and mitigated by testing several prefix lengths). Internal-entropy may correlate with plain difficulty, making it hard to distinguish from difficulty-probe baselines; the causal steering experiment and the direct comparison to difficulty probes address this. Steering entropy could produce degenerate or off-distribution samples, complicating the causal claim; we report sample validity. The allocator's gains may not exceed strong latent-predictor baselines like T2, but the mechanistic grounding (branch-worthiness as internal candidate-distribution entropy, causally validated) is a standalone contribution. Ground-truth utility estimation with k up to 16 is compute-intensive; we subsample problems and use smaller k for larger models. Results may be model-family and scale specific."}

