Response Content: {"total": 4, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "ab60ca888dfe60bc7a50f47bd483737523943682", "title": "Thought Anchors: Which LLM Reasoning Steps Matter?", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2025, "citationCount": 124, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.19143, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "importance_precommitment_gap",
  "Title": "Does a Model Know a Step Matters Before It Writes It? A Pre-Emission Importance Signal for Cheap, Faithful Reasoning Compression",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Reasoning models linearly encode, at the moment they begin a reasoning step, a pre-emission importance signal that predicts whether the step will turn out to be causally load-bearing (high counterfactual importance / 'thought anchor') or negligible. Because this signal is available before the step is generated, it can drive a training-free, attention-matrix-free budget allocator that spends full detail on anchor steps and compresses the rest, achieving better token-accuracy tradeoffs than post-hoc attention-based pruning.",
  "Related Work": "Thought Anchors (2025) and Can Aha Moments Be Fake?/TTS (2025) show only a small fraction of reasoning steps are causally load-bearing, but they measure importance retrospectively via resampling or corruption of already-generated steps. MI-Peaks (2025) finds 'thinking tokens' are information peaks, again identified during/after generation. Attention-based efficiency methods (DynTS, FROST, LazyEviction, G-KV, EpiKV 2026) prune or evict tokens using attention or representation-change signals computed after tokens exist; EpiKV notably argues attention is a noisy, expensive importance proxy and scores tokens by forward-pass representation change post-hoc. Preplan-and-Anchor (2025) uses attention to find anchors but for RL credit assignment, not prospective prediction. Crucially, NO prior work asks whether step importance is encoded PROSPECTIVELY, before the step is emitted, from a single forward pass at the step boundary, nor uses such a signal to allocate generation-time detail budget. Our contribution is (1) establishing that a cheap, attention-free, pre-emission linear probe predicts a step's eventual thought-anchor status, (2) causally validating it, and (3) turning it into a proactive detail-budget allocator that avoids materializing the attention matrix, unlike all attention-based baselines.",
  "Abstract": "Recent interpretability work shows that only a small minority of reasoning steps in a chain of thought are causally load-bearing 'thought anchors'; the rest are near-negligible. But every existing method identifies these anchors retrospectively, after the steps are generated, using resampling, corruption, or attention scores. We ask a sharper, forward-looking question: does a reasoning model already know, at the instant it starts a step, whether that step will matter? We hypothesize that the residual stream at a step boundary linearly encodes a pre-emission importance signal predicting the step's eventual counterfactual importance. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we first compute ground-truth per-step importance via resampling (thought-anchor counterfactual importance), then train a lightweight linear probe on pre-step activations to predict it, comparing against post-hoc attention-based and representation-change scores. We test whether the signal is causal by steering it and observing changes in the actual importance/length of the emitted step, and by localizing the components carrying it. We then convert the probe into a training-free, attention-matrix-free detail-budget allocator: at each step boundary the model spends a full-detail budget on predicted-anchor steps and a compressed budget (via prompted terseness or truncation-and-continue) on the rest. We evaluate token-accuracy tradeoffs against uniform compression, attention-based pruning (FROST/DynTS-style), and reactive eviction. Our work reframes efficient reasoning as prospective importance prediction and yields a cheap, deployable, interpretable allocator.",
  "Experiments": "1) Ground-truth step importance: Segment CoT into sentence-level steps. For each step, compute counterfactual importance via resampling (a la Thought Anchors): repeatedly sample semantically-different replacement steps, continue generation, and measure the shift in the final-answer distribution; also compute a TTS-style corruption score as a second label. Threshold to label steps anchor vs negligible. Report anchor sparsity across datasets/model sizes. 2) Pre-emission probing (core test): At each step boundary, extract residual-stream activations BEFORE the step is generated. Train per-layer linear probes to predict the step's ground-truth importance label. Metrics: AUROC/accuracy for prospective prediction; sweep layers to localize the signal. Baselines: (a) bag-of-words on prior text, (b) a retrospective probe that sees the generated step, (c) post-hoc attention-received score, (d) EpiKV-style representation-change score. Key claim: cheap pre-emission probe rivals or beats post-hoc scores that require the step to exist. 3) Causal validation: Steer along the probe direction (up/down) at a step boundary and measure whether the emitted step's realized importance (resampling score) and length change accordingly, versus random/control directions. Localize contributing components via activation patching. 4) Detail-budget allocator (training-free, attention-free): During generation, at each step boundary read the pre-emission importance; if predicted-anchor, allow full generation; if predicted-negligible, apply a compressed budget (inject a terseness instruction, cap step length, or skip-and-continue). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, uniform terseness, fixed truncation, FROST/DynTS-style attention pruning, EpiKV/LazyEviction reactive eviction. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup, and whether the allocator avoids materializing the attention matrix (deployability). 5) Faithfulness/failure analysis: Verify anchor steps are preserved (recall of true anchors), measure accuracy drop from mis-compressing a true anchor, and analyze behavior near the end of the trace. 6) Generalization: transfer probe and allocator across datasets and model scales without retraining.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Step importance may only be decidable mid-step rather than at the boundary, making pre-emission prediction weak; this would be an informative negative result and is partly mitigated by testing multiple boundary positions (first few tokens of a step). Resampling-based importance labels are noisy and compute-heavy; we subsample steps and use two labeling schemes for robustness. The signal may correlate with generic difficulty/position, blurring the distinction from simpler heuristics; we control with baselines and causal steering. Compressing predicted-negligible steps could still cascade errors on hard problems (underthinking); the anchor-recall metric and budget thresholds address this. Allocator gains may not exceed strong attention-based baselines, but the prospective, attention-free framing and its deployability (no attention-matrix materialization) are standalone contributions. Results may be model-family and scale specific."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'importance_precommitment_gap', 'Title': 'Does a Model Know a Step Matters Before It Writes It? A Pre-Emission Importance Signal for Cheap, Faithful Reasoning Compression', 'Short Hypothesis': "Reasoning models linearly encode, at the moment they begin a reasoning step, a pre-emission importance signal that predicts whether the step will turn out to be causally load-bearing (high counterfactual importance / 'thought anchor') or negligible. Because this signal is available before the step is generated, it can drive a training-free, attention-matrix-free budget allocator that spends full detail on anchor steps and compresses the rest, achieving better token-accuracy tradeoffs than post-hoc attention-based pruning.", 'Related Work': "Thought Anchors (2025) and Can Aha Moments Be Fake?/TTS (2025) show only a small fraction of reasoning steps are causally load-bearing, but they measure importance retrospectively via resampling or corruption of already-generated steps. MI-Peaks (2025) finds 'thinking tokens' are information peaks, again identified during/after generation. Attention-based efficiency methods (DynTS, FROST, LazyEviction, G-KV, EpiKV 2026) prune or evict tokens using attention or representation-change signals computed after tokens exist; EpiKV notably argues attention is a noisy, expensive importance proxy and scores tokens by forward-pass representation change post-hoc. Preplan-and-Anchor (2025) uses attention to find anchors but for RL credit assignment, not prospective prediction. Crucially, NO prior work asks whether step importance is encoded PROSPECTIVELY, before the step is emitted, from a single forward pass at the step boundary, nor uses such a signal to allocate generation-time detail budget. Our contribution is (1) establishing that a cheap, attention-free, pre-emission linear probe predicts a step's eventual thought-anchor status, (2) causally validating it, and (3) turning it into a proactive detail-budget allocator that avoids materializing the attention matrix, unlike all attention-based baselines.", 'Abstract': "Recent interpretability work shows that only a small minority of reasoning steps in a chain of thought are causally load-bearing 'thought anchors'; the rest are near-negligible. But every existing method identifies these anchors retrospectively, after the steps are generated, using resampling, corruption, or attention scores. We ask a sharper, forward-looking question: does a reasoning model already know, at the instant it starts a step, whether that step will matter? We hypothesize that the residual stream at a step boundary linearly encodes a pre-emission importance signal predicting the step's eventual counterfactual importance. Across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3), we first compute ground-truth per-step importance via resampling (thought-anchor counterfactual importance), then train a lightweight linear probe on pre-step activations to predict it, comparing against post-hoc attention-based and representation-change scores. We test whether the signal is causal by steering it and observing changes in the actual importance/length of the emitted step, and by localizing the components carrying it. We then convert the probe into a training-free, attention-matrix-free detail-budget allocator: at each step boundary the model spends a full-detail budget on predicted-anchor steps and a compressed budget (via prompted terseness or truncation-and-continue) on the rest. We evaluate token-accuracy tradeoffs against uniform compression, attention-based pruning (FROST/DynTS-style), and reactive eviction. Our work reframes efficient reasoning as prospective importance prediction and yields a cheap, deployable, interpretable allocator.", 'Experiments': "1) Ground-truth step importance: Segment CoT into sentence-level steps. For each step, compute counterfactual importance via resampling (a la Thought Anchors): repeatedly sample semantically-different replacement steps, continue generation, and measure the shift in the final-answer distribution; also compute a TTS-style corruption score as a second label. Threshold to label steps anchor vs negligible. Report anchor sparsity across datasets/model sizes. 2) Pre-emission probing (core test): At each step boundary, extract residual-stream activations BEFORE the step is generated. Train per-layer linear probes to predict the step's ground-truth importance label. Metrics: AUROC/accuracy for prospective prediction; sweep layers to localize the signal. Baselines: (a) bag-of-words on prior text, (b) a retrospective probe that sees the generated step, (c) post-hoc attention-received score, (d) EpiKV-style representation-change score. Key claim: cheap pre-emission probe rivals or beats post-hoc scores that require the step to exist. 3) Causal validation: Steer along the probe direction (up/down) at a step boundary and measure whether the emitted step's realized importance (resampling score) and length change accordingly, versus random/control directions. Localize contributing components via activation patching. 4) Detail-budget allocator (training-free, attention-free): During generation, at each step boundary read the pre-emission importance; if predicted-anchor, allow full generation; if predicted-negligible, apply a compressed budget (inject a terseness instruction, cap step length, or skip-and-continue). Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Baselines: no intervention, uniform terseness, fixed truncation, FROST/DynTS-style attention pruning, EpiKV/LazyEviction reactive eviction. Metrics: accuracy, mean tokens, token-reduction-at-equal-accuracy, wall-clock speedup, and whether the allocator avoids materializing the attention matrix (deployability). 5) Faithfulness/failure analysis: Verify anchor steps are preserved (recall of true anchors), measure accuracy drop from mis-compressing a true anchor, and analyze behavior near the end of the trace. 6) Generalization: transfer probe and allocator across datasets and model scales without retraining.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'Step importance may only be decidable mid-step rather than at the boundary, making pre-emission prediction weak; this would be an informative negative result and is partly mitigated by testing multiple boundary positions (first few tokens of a step). Resampling-based importance labels are noisy and compute-heavy; we subsample steps and use two labeling schemes for robustness. The signal may correlate with generic difficulty/position, blurring the distinction from simpler heuristics; we control with baselines and causal steering. Compressing predicted-negligible steps could still cascade errors on hard problems (underthinking); the anchor-recall metric and budget thresholds address this. Allocator gains may not exceed strong attention-based baselines, but the prospective, attention-free framing and its deployability (no attention-matrix materialization) are standalone contributions. Results may be model-family and scale specific.'}

