Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "shallow_token_depth_skip",
"Title": "Most Reasoning Tokens Are Shallow: Training-Free Adaptive Depth via a Cheap Deep-Thinking Predictor",
"Short Hypothesis": "In a reasoning model's chain of thought, the vast majority of generated tokens (connectives, narration, formatting, easy continuations) reach their final prediction in early-to-middle layers ('shallow tokens'), while only a sparse set of 'deep-thinking' tokens require the full stack. Whether the current token is shallow is predictable online from mid-layer activations, so a training-free adaptive-depth decoder can early-exit the shallow majority through the LM head while running full depth only on deep tokens, cutting compute at fixed accuracy.",
"Related Work": "Adaptive-depth architectures (Mixture-of-Depths, Mixture-of-Recursions, Inner Thinking Transformer, Think-at-Hard, adaptive latent CoT) all REQUIRE training routers/adapters from scratch or fine-tuning. Interpretability work on depth usage (Guess-then-Refine 2025; Concept Depth 2024; Demystifying LLM Layers 2025) shows tokens converge at different depths but does not build a deployable decoder. 'Think Deep, Not Just Long' (2026) measures deep-thinking tokens (predictions revised in deep layers) and uses the deep-thinking ratio to SELECT good samples (test-time scaling), not to allocate per-token depth during generation. MTD/Divergence Steering (2025) measures computation density via a shallow-head KL and steers text style, not depth-skipping. KV-cache pruning (DynTS, EpiKV, LazyEviction) reduces memory over the sequence dimension, orthogonal to skipping layers on the current token. Classic early-exit LMs (e.g., CALM) exist for general LM but rely on confidence thresholds and typically need calibration training; none exploit the specific mechanistic structure that reasoning CoTs are dominated by shallow narration tokens, nor characterize which token TYPES are deep. Our contribution is threefold and distinct: (1) a mechanistic characterization that deep-thinking need in reasoning CoT is sparse, structured (concentrated on computation/answer tokens, not narration), and cheaply predictable mid-stack; (2) a fully training-free adaptive-depth decoder for existing reasoning models using a self-speculative check; (3) an efficiency evaluation showing token-wise depth skipping is complementary to step-level stopping methods.",
"Abstract": "Reasoning language models generate long chains of thought, but not every token is computationally hard. Building on the 'guess-then-refine' view of transformer depth, we hypothesize that in reasoning traces the overwhelming majority of tokens\u2014connectives, narration, formatting, and easy continuations\u2014converge to their final prediction in early-to-middle layers, while only a sparse set of 'deep-thinking' tokens (arithmetic results, logical commitments, answer tokens) need the full stack. We first characterize this structure across GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, and GPQA on open reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1 distills 1.5B-14B, Qwen3): using logit-lens saturation and prediction-revision-in-deep-layers, we measure the per-token depth at which the argmax stabilizes and show deep-thinking need is sparse and concentrated on specific token categories rather than narration. We then test whether a token's deep-thinking need is decidable ONLINE from mid-layer signals, comparing a cheap mid-layer confidence/agreement check and a lightweight linear probe. Finally we build a fully training-free adaptive-depth decoder: at each token, run to a mid-layer checkpoint, and if the checkpoint prediction is confidently stable, exit through the LM head, skipping the remaining layers; otherwise complete full depth (a self-speculative verify step guards accuracy). We evaluate compute-at-fixed-accuracy against uniform full depth, fixed-layer early exit, and CALM-style confidence exit, and show the method is complementary to step-level early stopping. Our work turns a mechanistic depth observation into a deployable, interpretable efficiency mechanism for reasoning.",
"Experiments": "1) Per-token depth characterization: For sampled reasoning traces, apply logit-lens at every layer and record the 'convergence depth' = earliest layer after which the argmax token equals the final output and stays stable. Report the distribution of convergence depths; quantify the fraction of tokens that converge by layer L/2, 2L/3, etc. Categorize tokens (function/connective, narration, numeric/operator, answer-region) via POS tagging + regex and show deep-thinking (late-converging) tokens are concentrated in numeric/logical/answer categories, not narration. Cross-check with the 'prediction-revised-in-deep-layers' definition from Think Deep. 2) Online decidability: Test whether deep-thinking need can be predicted from mid-layer state alone, using (a) mid-layer logit-lens top-1 margin/entropy and top-1 agreement with an adjacent layer (training-free), and (b) a lightweight linear probe on mid-layer residual predicting whether the token will change by the final layer. Metric: AUROC/accuracy of predicting late-convergence; compare probe vs. confidence baselines; sweep checkpoint layer. 3) Adaptive-depth decoder (training-free): Implement decoding that runs to a checkpoint layer c; if the checkpoint's LM-head prediction is confident (margin > tau) and agrees with layer c-1, exit and emit; otherwise continue to full depth. Add an optional self-speculative safeguard: periodically verify a batch of exited tokens with full depth and adjust tau. Evaluate on GSM8K, MATH500, AIME, GPQA. Metrics: accuracy, average layers executed per token (FLOP proxy), wall-clock speedup, fraction of tokens early-exited. Baselines: uniform full depth, fixed truncation at c layers for all tokens, CALM-style confidence early exit, random exit at matched rate. 4) Category-aware ablation: verify early-exited tokens are dominantly narration/connective and that numeric/answer tokens rarely exit early; measure accuracy damage when we force early exit on answer-region tokens (should be large), validating the mechanistic claim causally. 5) Complementarity: combine token-wise depth skipping with a step-level early-stopping baseline and measure additive compute savings on the accuracy-compute Pareto frontier. 6) Sensitivity/failure analysis: sweep tau and checkpoint layer to trace the accuracy-vs-compute curve; report accuracy drop on hardest items (AIME) and underthinking risk; measure calibration drift across datasets to test transfer without retuning. 7) Generalization: transfer the probe and thresholds across datasets and model scales without retraining.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "Standard transformer forward passes are not trivially layer-skippable at decode time because later layers' KV entries would be missing for future attention; we mitigate by exiting only the current token's residual through the head while still computing (or approximating/caching) the KV of skipped layers\u2014this may limit realized speedup and must be measured carefully (we report both a FLOP proxy and true wall-clock, and test a variant that reuses checkpoint-layer KV). If KV inconsistency degrades downstream attention, gains may shrink (an informative systems finding). Deep-thinking need may be only weakly predictable at the checkpoint (the model may decide deep), yielding modest exit rates\u2014still a publishable characterization. Logit-lens convergence depth is a proxy for computational need and may misclassify tokens; we cross-validate with two definitions. Forcing early exit could cascade errors on hard problems; tau sweeps and the self-speculative safeguard address this. Gains may not exceed CALM-style baselines, but the mechanistic characterization (sparse, category-structured, mid-stack-predictable deep-thinking in reasoning CoT) and its training-free, complementary deployment are standalone contributions. Results may be model-family and scale specific."
}}
